WEBVTT

00:00:06.303 --> 00:00:15.209
Formulas let a report calculate or transform a displayed value. Conditions control when an element appears. We will use both on a fictional invoice.

00:00:20.017 --> 00:00:26.326
Open the invoice example and select the customer name. In Content, switch the value source to Formula.

00:00:33.871 --> 00:00:46.501
This value formula accepts a safe if/return block. For Alex Morgan, show N/A; otherwise keep the customer name. The report expression engine evaluates this block without running arbitrary JavaScript.

00:00:48.596 --> 00:00:55.470
The canvas now shows N/A for this sample customer. Change the formula condition or sample data to see the other result.

00:01:01.584 --> 00:01:13.266
Select the invoice date. Its formula uses the built-in formatDate function; change the format to show a long month and year. The supported functions and formats are available in the editor suggestions and guide.

00:01:31.166 --> 00:01:38.924
Select the status text and open Rules. Turn on visibility, then use the builder to show it only when the invoice status equals Due.

00:01:43.204 --> 00:01:55.035
The Code view shows the equivalent boolean expression. You can write supported expressions here, but this is not a full JavaScript runtime: no imports, assignments, loops or arbitrary functions.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:06.801
Preview the PDF to verify the transformed name, readable date and conditional status in the finished report.
